WEBVTT

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.910
Cyber threat intelligence, often shortened to CTI,

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:07.075
is analyzed and contextual information about threats that supports security decisions.

00:00:07.075 --> 00:00:08.500
The important word is analyzed.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:12.000
A log line, an alert, a suspicious domain, a malware hash,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.135
or a vendor feed entry can be useful input, but it is not automatically intelligence.

00:00:16.135 --> 00:00:19.000
Intelligence explains what the information may mean,

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.965
why it matters to a specific environment, how confident the analyst is,

00:00:21.965 --> 00:00:23.933
and what decision or action it should support.

00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:27.995
A common beginner mistake is to treat more indicators as more intelligence.

00:00:27.995 --> 00:00:31.520
A long blocklist can look impressive, but if the entries are stale, duplicated,

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:33.995
unrelated to the organization, or missing context,

00:00:33.995 --> 00:00:36.075
they may create noise rather than protection.

00:00:36.075 --> 00:00:40.090
The same is true for rumors, headlines, screenshots, and unreviewed threat feeds.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.310
They may point toward something worth investigating,

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:45.135
but they need evaluation before they become a useful product.

00:00:45.135 --> 00:00:46.886
Good CTI starts with a question.

00:00:46.886 --> 00:00:50.965
A security operations team might ask whether a new phishing campaign affects the company.

00:00:50.965 --> 00:00:54.785
A vulnerability management team might ask whether a newly disclosed flaw is being

00:00:54.785 --> 00:00:57.015
exploited against similar organizations.

00:00:57.015 --> 00:01:00.995
An incident responder might ask which systems to scope after a confirmed intrusion.

00:01:00.995 --> 00:01:04.120
A leader might ask whether a geopolitical event changes business risk.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.595
Each question needs a different level of detail, different sources,

00:01:07.595 --> 00:01:09.135
and a different format.

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:10.000
Consider a simple example.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.045
A feed entry says that a domain was associated with credential phishing last month.

00:01:14.045 --> 00:01:16.045
By itself, that is raw threat information.

00:01:16.045 --> 00:01:19.370
It becomes more useful when an analyst checks whether the domain is still active,

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.995
whether the organization has seen related email messages,

00:01:21.995 --> 00:01:25.995
whether employees visited it, whether similar domains are being registered,

00:01:25.995 --> 00:01:29.080
and whether existing controls would detect or block the activity.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.995
The intelligence product might recommend a detection update, a targeted search,

00:01:32.995 --> 00:01:35.100
or no action because the item is stale and irrelevant.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.410
Useful intelligence is relevant, timely, accurate enough for the decision,

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.135
and actionable for the audience.

00:01:40.135 --> 00:01:47.995
Accurate enough does not mean perfect.

00:01:47.995 --> 00:01:48.000
CTI work often happens with partial information, so the analyst must explain confidence,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:48.045
assumptions, and limits.

00:01:48.045 --> 00:01:51.790
If intelligence cannot influence a decision, improve detection, prioritize response,

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:55.040
enrich an investigation, guide a hunt, or inform risk owners,

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:56.385
it may be interesting information,

00:01:56.385 --> 00:01:58.045
but it is not yet a defensive capability.

00:01:58.045 --> 00:01:58.620
In practice,

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.445
threat intelligence is a disciplined bridge between threat information and security

00:02:02.445 --> 00:02:03.065
action.

00:02:03.065 --> 00:02:07.010
It helps teams decide what to monitor, what to block, what to hunt for,

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.955
what to patch first, what to brief to leadership,

00:02:09.955 --> 00:02:12.065
and what evidence to preserve during incident response.

00:02:12.065 --> 00:02:13.000
The value is not the feed.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.000
The value is the decision support created from trustworthy collection, careful analysis,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.990
clear dissemination, and feedback from the people using the result.
